# Interactive cursor affordance

All clickable controls must show `cursor: pointer` on hover. Global styles in `web/src/styles/global.css` cover native elements (`button`, `a[href]`, `[role="button"]`, etc.) — do not rely on that alone for custom interactive UI.

## When building interactive elements

- **Native controls** — use `<button>`, `<a href>`, or appropriate `role` + keyboard handlers; global CSS applies pointer automatically.
- **Custom click targets** — add `cursor-pointer` in Tailwind (close buttons, icon toggles, card links, carousel slides).
- **Disabled state** — use `disabled` or `aria-disabled="true"` so global `cursor: not-allowed` applies; never leave disabled controls with pointer.

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — close control (global CSS covers native button)
<button
  type="button"
  onClick={onClose}
  className="flex size-7 items-center justify-center rounded-full hover:bg-neutral-100"
  aria-label="Close"
>
  <CloseIcon />
</button>

// ✅ GOOD — non-button interactive surface
<div
  role="button"
  tabIndex={0}
  onClick={onSelect}
  onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}
  className="cursor-pointer"
>

// ❌ BAD — div with only onClick, no role or cursor
<div onClick={onClose} className="size-7 rounded-full">
```

## Covered by global CSS (no extra class needed)

`a[href]`, `button`, `summary`, `label[for]`, form inputs, `[role="button"]`, `[role="link"]`, `[role="menuitem"]`, `[role="tab"]`.
